{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"b6aa40ffd31ea86d","type":"text","text":"# Observing analysis output\nRunning `uv run pne-analyze examples/integer_math.py:add -j`\n(-j for json output, :add to select only that function from the file) gives this result:\n```json\n{\n  \"FUNCTION_SCHEMAS\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"add\", \n      \"callableId\": \"5a01db262e21bbbc\",\n      \"category\": [\"examples\", \"integer_math\"],\n      \"definitionPath\": \"Users/tristanryerparke/python-node-editor/examples/integer_math.py:1\"\n      \"doc\": \"Adds two numbers together and returns the result\",\n      \"arguments\": {\n        \"a\": {\"type\": \"int\", \"value\": null},\n        \"b\": {\"type\": \"int\", \"value\": null}\n      },\n      \"outputs\": {\n        \"return\": {\"type\": \"int\", \"value\": null}\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"TYPES\": {\n    \"int\": {\"kind\": \"builtin\"}\n  }\n}\n```\nSo what are these fields:\n- `FUNCTION_SCHEMAS` is a list of the functions discovered by PNE\n- `TYPES` is a dict of the types used in the input and output arguments of your function","x":-1320,"y":-260,"width":640,"height":820},
		{"id":"54b5a5fdd7098341","type":"text","text":"# Starting from a simple typed function\n\nIf you write a typed python function in a file, you can direct PNE at that file and it will analyze the functions inside. \n\n```python\n# examples/integer_math.py\ndef add(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n\t\"\"\"adds two numbers together\"\"\"\n    return a + b\n```","x":-2000,"y":-10,"width":460,"height":300},
		{"id":"5a94009bb86a90f6","type":"text","text":"# Function Schema\n- `name`: The python name of the function.\n- `callableId`: Is the first 16 characters of an sha256 hash of the function's source code.\n-  `category`: A clean representation of where the function is located with respect to the working directory.\n- `definitionPath`: Full path to where the function is defined, including line number.\n- `doc`: The function's docstring\n- `arguments`: The arguments defined, each with a `type` parameter corresponding to a value in `TYPES`\n- `outputs`: A similar definition to `arguments` for the return type\n\nThis data is then sent up to the frontend to automatically construct a \"node\" for your function so you can use populate and execute it in the GUI","x":-420,"y":-320,"width":620,"height":420},
		{"id":"a3a633bee12e791c","type":"text","text":"# Type Schemas\nThe type schema, at least with basic functions like *add* is very simple. The `kind` field marks it as a builtin python type (one that is supported by PNE).\n\nFor a divide function like this:\n```python\ndef divide(a: int, b: int) -> float:\n\treturn a / b\n```\nYou would get int and float:\n```json\n\"TYPES\": {\n  \"int\": {\"kind\": \"builtin\"},\n  \"float\": {\"kind\": \"builtin\"}\n}\n```\nFor more complicated types (user types/classes, list, dict) etc this schema will have more structure.\n","x":-420,"y":140,"width":620,"height":500},
		{"id":"d23e5e51230e2d5b","x":-420,"y":760,"width":620,"height":480,"type":"text","text":"# Dealing with list/dict"}
	],
